/// FINDING (v-compiler-ml stress probe, 2026-07-15): a CHECK/COMPILE DIVERGENCE for `Set.to-list` over an
/// empty set literal whose ELEMENT TYPE is undetermined.
///
///   def es() = Set.of([])                    // empty set literal — element type unconstrained
///   def run() = List.len(Set.to-list(es()))  // Set.to-list needs an ordering descriptor for the elem type
///
/// `cdz check`   → EXIT 0, NO diagnostic (the type-checker accepts it).
/// `cdz compile` → FAILS: "Set.to-list element shape has no orderable descriptor" (no CDZ code; an
///                 internal-sounding message, not an actionable one).
///
/// So a program the CHECKER accepts, the BACKEND rejects — a reject-don't-diverge violation. The root: an
/// empty `Set.of([])` leaves the element type undetermined; `Set.to-list` must bake a canonical-ordering
/// shape descriptor from that type, which needs a concrete element type. Two acceptable fixes:
///   (a) the CHECKER rejects `Set.to-list` (or the un-annotated empty `Set.of([])` reaching it) with a
///       clear, coded diagnostic — e.g. "cannot determine the element type of an empty set; annotate it";
///   (b) `Set.to-list` on an under-determined element declines with an actionable message, not the raw
///       "no orderable descriptor".
///
/// DISCRIMINATORS (all verified 2026-07-15):
///   • `Set.of([])` + `Set.len`   → COMPILES + runs (0). `len` needs no element type, so the empty literal
///     alone is fine; only `to-list`'s ordering-descriptor bake needs the concrete type.
///   • a runtime-empty but TYPED set — `Set.to-list(Set.remove(Set.of([1]), 1))` → COMPILES + runs (0).
///     So it is the UNDETERMINED element type of the empty LITERAL, not "empty at runtime", that trips it.
///   • `Set.to-list(Set.of([3,1,2]))` → COMPILES + runs (3, canonical order). Non-empty literal is fine.
def es() = Set.of([])
def run() = List.len(Set.to-list(es()))
export { run }

